{"skill":{"slug":"vmware-monitor","displayName":"vmware-monitor"},"version":{"version":"2.4.2","createdAt":1792368941745,"changelog":"An unreadable host is not reported as down (hosts_unreadable); NTP/inventory no longer call it unreachable; a login refused with NoPermission gets the no-role error","changelogSource":"user","license":"MIT-0","files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":25070,"sha256":"0ff359cc0c8b0622ed60f7ce5188e0def3533f41416903440ceb6f8d0407f336","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"evals/evals.json","size":1255,"sha256":"3b2746e29a48a3ff97d6c33c40eea6c79b26d8b6e2e1fbacfb7bd52518f4a8b4","contentType":"application/json"},{"path":"references/agent-guardrails.md","size":6781,"sha256":"9d524072391e27c821f7053a08d2342e1a8487a19d63cd51a4ae91cba9b33e6c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/capabilities.md","size":22284,"sha256":"460fcdbae81b60489ab47321750a50a590fca2dd5a50be04d39ab45064340fbe","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/cli-reference.md","size":12459,"sha256":"23e4c9301c18270603e5fa05892c787c6251d599294264522e06cd598c194487","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/health-summary-template.md","size":9143,"sha256":"5209bb6cda1ba7af7e3d0f5c46854e92774fed961c50c26758aa4ee5d4affca5","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/investigation-protocol.md","size":6758,"sha256":"159bdcc5dfdf9b6414c548d7bf7c326dbb849e38883699a4a80cb76267c55893","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/setup-guide.md","size":18033,"sha256":"61ca7ba6f75ae0e9c83fe7769fc21c27c81bf144be663a6844c24d767ea341b2","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2048,"sha256":"947a10893d4eef70e3f8c39b2ad3345d1b2d83fe66baa49335b6007d61eebfe5","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"clean","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1792369659481,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"0084de735254b9d3af0b081210f106577fdf44c298e46737616fa29fa41e0e3c","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/0084de735254b9d3af0b081210f106577fdf44c298e46737616fa29fa41e0e3c","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":null,"normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":null,"source":"engines","checkedAt":1792369307528},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":44,"checkedAt":1792369659481},"llm":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","confidence":"high","summary":"This is a clearly disclosed VMware monitoring skill with sensitive but purpose-aligned read-only infrastructure access and optional user-started notifications.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The skill can reveal sensitive infrastructure inventory, events, logs, sessions, hostnames, IPs, and usernames, but this is expected for VMware monitoring and is disclosed with least-privilege guidance.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"Installation uses the normal uv/PyPI package path and documentation includes pinned examples; the skill relies on an external Python package rather than bundling implementation code in the artifact.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The routing language includes broad phrases such as \"is anything on fire,\" but the surrounding context ties them to VMware infrastructure triage and repeatedly excludes vSphere write operations.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"Local config, credential, audit, snapshot, and daemon files are disclosed; the daemon and Slack/Discord webhooks are opt-in and user-started, with no autostart behavior shown.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The stated purpose is read-only VMware vCenter/ESXi monitoring, and the documented capabilities align with that purpose: inventory, alarms, events, performance, capacity, sessions, host logs, health summaries, and investigations.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"ok"}],"guidance":"Install with a dedicated read-only VMware account, avoid administrator credentials, prefer a secret manager over storing passwords in .env, and enable webhooks or the daemon only when you are comfortable sending operational details such as hostnames, IPs, usernames, alarms, and event text to that destination.","findings":"[PE3] expected: The .env credential storage finding is real, but it is disclosed as required for per-target VMware authentication, restricted to chmod 600, labeled as obfuscation rather than encryption, and accompanied by secret-manager guidance.\n[TM1] expected: The --skip-auth flag is documented only for config parsing diagnostics without connecting; it does not bypass access to vCenter data.\n[TM3] expected: The same --skip-auth evidence does not indicate an unsafe default, because authentication remains the normal diagnostic path and the flag is explicit.\n[RA1] unexpected: The cited text discusses labels used by separate write-capable VMware skills and does not show this skill modifying itself or changing its own behavior at runtime.\n[AS1] expected: The reference to ~/.claude/settings.json is an instruction for users to add MCP configuration; it does not instruct the skill to read agent configuration directories.\n[SQP-1] expected: The broad trigger phrases are somewhat loose, but they are embedded in VMware-specific monitoring instructions and lead to read-only checks.\n[RA2] expected: Local state files and daemon files are disclosed and tied to user-run setup, audit logging, snapshots, or the explicit daemon start command; no hidden cross-session persistence is shown.\n[EA2] expected: Automatic execution without confirmation is scoped to read-only L1/L2 monitoring and analysis; remediation is explicitly out of scope or routed to companion skills requiring separate handling.\n[SDI-1] expected: Host-log scanning and the scheduled daemon are additional monitoring surfaces, but they are explicitly documented in the main skill and references as scanning and webhook capabilities.\n[SDI-2] expected: ESXi log inspection is broader than simple inventory, but it is directly relevant to operational monitoring and is disclosed with privilege requirements.\n[RP1] expected: The npx installer path carries ordinary supply-chain risk, but the setup guide pins the GitHub revision and also provides a pinned uv install path; this is not evidence of malicious behavior.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1792369659481}}}}}